    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Orders and growth hold up, but a $50M share-sale plan sinks OSS to 52-week low",
               "technical":  "OSS closed at 4.01 USD on 2026-11-20, down 7.82% on the day. The session low of 3.88 is now the 52-week low. The trend is clearly down. Price sits far below the EMA 50 (6.61), EMA 100 (8.47) and EMA 200 (9.28). EMAs are moving averages that weight recent prices more heavily and track the short-, medium- and long-term trend. All three are stacked in bearish order (50 \u003c 100 \u003c 200). Momentum is weak but the decline is slowing. MACD (12/26/9, a trend-momentum gauge) is -0.73, slightly above its signal line at -0.80, so the histogram is a small +0.07. That means selling pressure is fading, which is not the same as a reversal. RSI 14 is a 0–100 momentum gauge where readings under 30 count as oversold. It stands at 31.94, just above that zone. Price is in the lower half of the Bollinger Bands (20-day, 2 standard deviations: 3.39–6.38, middle 4.89) but still above the lower band. Volume was 1.61M shares, in line with the 20-day average of 1.60M, so the drop was not a high-volume capitulation. Support sits at 3.88, then 3.39. Resistance sits at 4.89, then 6.38.",
               "fundamental":  "OSS builds rugged GPU servers and PCIe expansion systems that put data-centre-class AI compute into aircraft, ships and ground vehicles. Its Q3 2026 results (reported 2026-10-28) showed strong demand. Continuing-operations revenue rose 35.9% to 12.6M USD, nine-month revenue rose 48.3% to 30.0M, and the year-to-date book-to-bill was 1.46 (orders divided by shipments). Full-year growth guidance went up to about 40% from 25–30%, which implies roughly 15M USD of Q4 revenue. The weak spots were profit quality and funding. Gross margin slipped to 42.6% from 45.6% and the GAAP net loss was 0.6M. Adjusted EBITDA was only 0.3M after a 0.5M bad-debt charge and 0.4M of legal fees. Nine-month operating cash burn was 12.4M, leaving 18.9M of cash and short-term investments. On the same day, OSS set up a 50M USD at-the-market (ATM) share-sale program.",
               "news":  [
                            "2026-10-26: OSS said its Gen5 4UP-MAX expansion system was qualified with an unnamed Tier 1 global enterprise computing platform provider. The system lets customers add up to eight AI accelerators to existing servers, though management called the opportunity early-stage.",
                            "2026-10-28: Q3 revenue grew 35.9% to 12.6M USD and FY2026 growth guidance was raised to about 40%. The stock still fell about 7.5% that day on lower gross margin, a bad-debt charge from a bankrupt former customer, and legal costs.",
                            "2026-10-28: OSS signed an equity distribution agreement with Piper Sandler to sell up to 50M USD of stock at market prices. The CFO said the company is not required to issue shares and that any money raised would fund organic investment and possible acquisitions.",
                            "2026-11-05: According to price-data sites, shares fell about 15.9% to close at 3.92 USD on above-average volume, and I found no company release that explains the move. After the results, Roth Capital cut its price target to 15 from 18 USD and kept its Buy rating.",
                            "2026-11-09: OSS won a 2.75M USD U.S. Army contract to develop a SOSA-aligned compute appliance for tactical ground vehicles, with working systems due in H2 2027. The stock rose about 17% that day.",
                            "2026-11-12: OSS received its 10th order since May 2025 from a leading defense and intelligence contractor, with possible further production orders in 2027–2028.",
                            "2026-11-20: The 7.82% drop to 4.01 USD came on average volume, and I found no company-specific news to explain it."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The 50M USD ATM program is the biggest overhang. It is large next to 18.9M USD of cash, and selling shares near a 52-week low would dilute existing holders heavily. That supply can also cap rallies. Management expects lower Q4 gross margin because of the production mix and more customer-funded development work, and full-year adjusted EBITDA is guided at only 0.5–1.0M USD. Individual contracts are small (1–3M USD) and lumpy. About 5.6M USD of milestone collections slipped from Q3 into October. A bad-debt charge and a 6.25M USD legal settlement show how much damage single customers can do. Price remains below all three EMAs.",
               "opportunity":  "Revenue is growing about 40% a year, and a 1.46 book-to-bill means orders are arriving faster than shipments. Management expects several multimillion-dollar orders to close in Q4. It also pointed to larger programs, not included in guidance, that could be worth 100M USD or more over 3–4 years if they move into funded production. The Army ground-vehicle development win and repeat orders from a defense-intelligence contractor support the niche rugged-compute moat. The Tier 1 qualification opens an enterprise sales channel. RSI near 30 and a MACD histogram that has turned positive suggest selling may be wearing out. A close back above 4.89 would be a first sign of repair.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Shareholder dilution if OSS sells stock through the 50M USD ATM at depressed prices",
                             "Lower gross margin in Q4 2026 and only break-even-level adjusted EBITDA",
                             "Lumpy, small defense contracts. Development awards may not turn into production orders",
                             "Customer concentration and credit risk (a customer bankruptcy and a 6.25M USD settlement in 2026)",
                             "Defense budget timing delays that push orders and collections into later quarters",
                             "High small-cap volatility: the stock is about 81% below its 52-week high of 20.88"
                         ],
               "summary":  "OSS is in a firm downtrend. It closed at 4.01 USD, below all three EMAs and right at its 52-week low, with RSI near oversold and MACD only hinting that losses are slowing. The business news is mixed rather than broken. Revenue grew 36%, guidance was raised, book-to-bill is 1.46, and new Army and defense-contractor orders arrived. However, thin margins, cash burn and the 50M USD ATM program have shifted the market\u0027s focus to dilution. The niche rugged-AI moat looks intact. The things to watch are Q4 order closings, margins and how much of the ATM gets used."
